  ▸ s281-D2 — RULED THE GUIDELINE FAMILY OWNS A DOUBLE-NAMED FILE, AND THE BUILDER DECLARES THE TIE: a file under knowledge/guidelines/ that is named BOTH by a ruling's artefacts (a `governs` entry in knowledge/_rulings.json) and by the rule index (knowledge/_rule_nodes.json) is a GUIDELINE-family node — `guidelinerules`, the Design-governance view — and NOT a Constitution node. The builder DECLARES the tie instead of resolving it by read order: both claimants are recorded on the node itself (`claimedBy: [governance, guidelinerules]`, with the file's kind and the tie's own sentence beside them, all readable in the node's INSPECT record), and the family is assigned by WHAT THE FILE IS, never by which pass ran first. The rule is GENERAL: every double-named file is declared, whatever its kind, and a kind the builder's kind map does not name keeps the family it has, is marked UNRESOLVED on the node and is PRINTED by the builder, so it becomes a question and never a silent decision. A ruling that names the file STILL DRAWS ITS ARTEFACT LINE TO IT — the `governs` edge keeps its own `governance` family and its target is unchanged, so the Constitution loses nothing by the re-homing. NO node id, edge type, file or fam KEY moves, no node is added or removed, no edge is invented, `obeys` and its `held` lines are not touched, and the page defaults are unchanged (`guidelinerules` still loads OFF). The baked coordinates of the two families whose membership changes DO move, and must: each family's column is its own force solution. (2026-09-17, Dave): Decisions export 2026-09-17T11:58:34.925Z, notes/_lanes/281/orphan-plan/DAVE-EXPORT-DECISIONS-2026-09-17.json, q2 = (a), note empty, recommended (a), overruled false. The question, verbatim: "When a ruling and a rule index both name the same guideline file, whose is it?" The option he chose, verbatim: "The guideline family owns it; the builder declares the tie instead of resolving it by read order". Earlier the same day, the orphan pl